[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Eloquent Performance: Eager Loading, N+1 Problem, and Chunks"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building robust applications in Laravel, Eloquent ORM is a powerful tool that simplifies database interactions. However, without careful optimization, database queries can quickly become a major performance bottleneck. This tutorial explores common performance pitfalls in Eloquent and how to fix them using Eager Loading, resolving the N+1 query problem, and processing large datasets efficiently with Chunks."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the N+1 Query Problem"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The N+1 query problem occurs when your code executes one query to retrieve the primary dataset (the 1), and then executes N additional queries to fetch related data for each individual record. This drastically increases the database load and degrades application response times."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// The N+1 Problem Example
$posts = Post::all();

foreach ($posts as $post) {
    // This executes a separate query for EVERY iteration
    echo $post->user->name;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Solving with Eager Loading"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To solve the N+1 problem, Laravel provides Eager Loading using the with method. This instructs Eloquent to load all required relationships in a single, highly efficient secondary query (or a SQL join, depending on how it is structured)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// The Eager Loading Solution
$posts = Post::with('user')->get();

foreach ($posts as $post) {
    // No extra queries are executed here
    echo $post->user->name;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use nested eager loading like Post::with('user.profile') to load relations of relations."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Constrain eager loads by passing closures to the with method to filter related data."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Processing Large Datasets with Chunks"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When dealing with thousands or millions of records, calling Post::all() or Post::get() will exhaust PHP's memory limit. The chunk method retrieves a small subset of records at a time and feeds them into a closure for processing."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"Post::chunk(200, function ($posts) {
    foreach ($posts as $post) {
        // Process each post without memory overflow
        $post->update(['status' => 'processed']);
    }
});"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Eager Loading to avoid the N+1 problem and Chunking for memory management, your Laravel applications will remain fast, scalable, and resource-efficient."}]
